MASTER PROMPT — CLEANHOME

Professional Home Cleaning Service Platform

1. Role and Project Objective

Act as a Senior Full-Stack Developer, UI/UX Designer, Application Security Engineer, and Database Architect.

Build a complete, professional, responsive, and secure home cleaning service website named CleanHome from scratch.

CleanHome is a home cleaning service platform that introduces the business to visitors, displays cleaning services, allows visitors to register and log in, and provides authenticated customers with a dashboard to explore services and manage their bookings.

IMPORTANT:

Start from a completely new project.

Do not continue, import, or depend on any previous CleanHome project, codebase, or authentication implementation.

Do not assume that existing files, components, database tables, or Supabase configurations exist.

Create all necessary application files, components, configurations, database migrations, security policies, and documentation.

Do not produce only a prototype, static mockup, or UI demonstration.

Implement real interactions and working functionality wherever specified.

Never claim that a feature works unless it has actually been implemented and tested.

The final result must resemble a real home cleaning service business, not a generic student dashboard.

2. Technology Stack

Use the following technology stack:

Frontend: React 19 + TypeScript.

Build Tool: Vite.

Styling: Tailwind CSS v4.

Routing: React Router.

Backend and Database: Supabase.

Authentication: Supabase Auth using @supabase/supabase-js.

Database: Supabase PostgreSQL.

Form Validation: Zod with React Hook Form where appropriate.

Icons: Lucide React.

Animation: Motion for React or a lightweight CSS-based animation solution.

Testing: Vitest and React Testing Library, with Playwright for essential end-to-end flows where practical.

Package Manager: npm.

Use stable, compatible package versions and follow the official documentation for the installed versions.

Do not introduce unnecessary frameworks, heavy UI libraries, or additional backend services without a clear reason.

Use TypeScript strict mode and avoid any unless there is a documented technical justification.

Supabase must be the real authentication and database provider, not a dummy service or hardcoded replacement.

