8. Supabase Database Architecture

Create the database schema from scratch.

Provide version-controlled SQL migration files inside a supabase/migrations/ directory.

At minimum, design the following tables:

A. profiles

Suggested fields:

id: UUID primary key referencing auth.users.id.

full_name.

phone.

address, if needed.

created_at.

updated_at.

A profile must belong to the corresponding authenticated user.

B. services

Suggested fields:

id: UUID primary key.

name.

slug, unique.

description.

price, using an appropriate numeric type.

image_url.

category.

is_active.

created_at.

updated_at.

Use appropriate constraints and indexes.

C. bookings

Suggested fields:

id: UUID primary key.

user_id: UUID referencing the authenticated user.

service_id: UUID referencing the selected service.

scheduled_at: timestamp with time zone.

service_address.

notes.

status.

created_at.

updated_at.

Use appropriate foreign keys, indexes, and constraints.

If booking prices must be preserved historically, implement a trusted price snapshot rather than relying on the current service price for every historical booking.

Do not expose unnecessary internal fields in public queries.

D. Database Security

Enable Row Level Security on every table containing application data accessible through the Supabase API.

Implement least-privilege policies.

Examples:

Public visitors can read only active service fields intended for public display.

Authenticated users can read and update only their own permitted profile fields.

Authenticated customers can read only their own bookings.

Customers can create bookings only for their own authenticated user ID.

Customers cannot assign privileged booking statuses.

Customers cannot change protected booking ownership fields.

Only explicitly authorized administrative processes can create or modify services and perform privileged booking transitions.

Do not rely on frontend route guards as a replacement for database authorization.

Use database constraints and trusted server-side logic where necessary to protect invariants that RLS alone cannot safely enforce.

Never create an unrestricted policy such as USING (true) for private profiles or customer bookings.

Avoid overly permissive WITH CHECK (true) policies.

If privileged service management is not part of this phase, do not introduce a public administrator account or hardcoded admin credentials.

E. Profile Creation

If using a database trigger to create profiles after registration:

Use a carefully scoped SECURITY DEFINER function only if necessary.

Set a safe and explicit search_path.

Qualify database object references.

Restrict function execution privileges.

Validate data appropriately.

Ensure malformed user metadata cannot create unauthorized privileges.

Never derive administrator privileges from user-editable registration metadata.

F. Seed Data

